WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.960
After watching this video, you walk into&nbsp;
your IELTS Speaking exam knowing exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:12.000
what's coming and how to give strong answers&nbsp;
in parts one, two, and three using the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.040
skills you already have. These strategies help&nbsp;
thousands of our students reach band seven, eight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:27.120
or even nine. And I'm going to share them in this&nbsp;
video. We'll start with part one at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.760
of your exam which you can fully predict. Yes,&nbsp;
I've taken the IELTS exam seven times and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:42.080
always the same. And the former examiners I work&nbsp;
with also confirm that in this part they basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:50.880
read from a script. So let me take you through&nbsp;
the beginning of your exam step by step. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:59.120
walk in, a quick hello or good morning is enough.&nbsp;
You sit down, the examiner reads a short recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:07.600
statement. This exam is recorded, their name,&nbsp;
the date, and so on. And then the identification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:15.280
questions begin. And one thing often surprises my&nbsp;
students about these answers. So, let me take you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:23.600
through each question and answer. Can you tell&nbsp;
me your full name, please? My name is Alexandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:32.560
Brown. What shall I call you? Please call me&nbsp;
Alex. Where are you from? I'm from Kazakhstan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:39.600
Can I see your identification? Yes, of course.&nbsp;
Here you are. Or here is my passport. So, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:47.280
quick things to remember. Say your name exactly as&nbsp;
it appears in your passport. Middle name included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:55.840
if it's in your passport and nothing added if it's&nbsp;
not. And the question, where are you from? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:03.840
it doesn't require a long answer. Remember,&nbsp;
this is just to identify you. And the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:11.360
exam questions that determine your score start&nbsp;
in a second. So, this whole part takes about 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:18.080
seconds. And the cleaner you do it, the more time&nbsp;
you save for the questions that actually matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:25.040
Part one of your IELTS speaking test lasts between&nbsp;
four and five minutes and includes between 10 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:32.720
12 questions on three different topics. And&nbsp;
the very first topic the examiner picks is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.360
one of four. So let me show you all of them.&nbsp;
After the idea questions, the examiner says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.920
"Now in this first part, I'd like to ask you&nbsp;
some questions about yourself. And your first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.560
topic will be one of these. Home, hometown,&nbsp;
work or studies. You get a choice between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.800
work and studies depending on what you're doing.&nbsp;
But the rest is picked for you. Let's go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:07.440
four sample answers. Two for opening questions and&nbsp;
two for follow-up questions. And each will teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:15.040
you a specific thing that will help you improve&nbsp;
your score. So for the topic of home, the opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:23.600
question is, do you live in a flat or a house?&nbsp;
And sometimes students answer with a single word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:31.760
a house. It's correct, but it's too short. The&nbsp;
examiner has nothing to assess. And the only extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:40.400
question they can ask you is why. And if they&nbsp;
keep on asking why, why, this isn't a great sign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:47.760
So here is a simple way to handle it. You give&nbsp;
your answer, you add a detail, and you briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:56.800
explain why yourself. So I live in a house with&nbsp;
my family. We moved there a few years ago because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:06.160
our old flat was a bit too small. This answer is&nbsp;
natural, complete, and it doesn't sound rehearsed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.680
which is very important. You don't want the&nbsp;
examiner to think you've learned your answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:21.760
by heart. They have to sound natural. Now, work or&nbsp;
studies. The opening question is, do you work or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:29.280
are you a student? And two things may weaken your&nbsp;
answer. So, one is if your answer is too short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:36.480
and we've just touched on that. And the other&nbsp;
is if you prepare your answer so thoroughly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:43.040
you really try to impress the examiner and you&nbsp;
give lots of special terms or you're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:51.440
say something smart, you shouldn't do that. So&nbsp;
here is what a strong answer sounds like. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:59.360
a student and actually I'm in the second year&nbsp;
of a bachelor's degree in computer science. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:06.000
the moment, we're doing a lot of programming and&nbsp;
problem solving, so it can be pretty challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:15.280
but I'm really enjoying it. You give your answer&nbsp;
and then you develop it a little bit. Yes, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:22.880
more than you would say in a normal conversation&nbsp;
by, hey, it's an IELTS exam. And if you work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:32.800
the same structure works for you. I work as what&nbsp;
I do and how I feel about it. Now the second and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:40.400
third questions in your topic are less predictable&nbsp;
and they will test different language skills. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:50.160
let me give you some common ones. Uh going back to&nbsp;
the topic of whom your next question may be what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:58.960
do you like best about your house? The temptation&nbsp;
may be to mention several things. I like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:07.520
bedroom and the garden and the neighborhood.&nbsp;
A list. This doesn't demonstrate your English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:17.520
skills in the best way. A better approach is&nbsp;
to pick one thing and expand it. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:25.040
what I like most is the natural light. The living&nbsp;
room has large windows, so it feels really bright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:32.960
and airy, especially in the morning. It just makes&nbsp;
the whole place feel more open and welcoming. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:39.920
you take one idea and you develop it naturally,&nbsp;
and it's a much stronger answer. Now, let's take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:48.240
a question that tests your ability to talk about&nbsp;
a hypothetical, unreal situation. Would you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:55.680
to live somewhere else in the future? A good way&nbsp;
to answer this question and to show how good your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:04.720
English is is to avoid saying yes, I will move&nbsp;
next year or something similar that sounds too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:14.480
simplistic unless it's true and you can back it&nbsp;
up. Otherwise, try to soften your language. Use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:24.080
words like possibly, maybe, in the long term, for&nbsp;
now. Like this. Possibly, yes. In the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:31.120
I'd like to live somewhere quieter, maybe in a&nbsp;
small town, because I like having more space and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:40.400
a slower pace. But for now, the city makes more&nbsp;
sense for work and opportunities. When you talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:48.880
about a possible future this way, you naturally&nbsp;
use more flexible and more complex grammar, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:56.320
the examiner will notice. All four answers we've&nbsp;
just looked at are around 20 to 25 seconds long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:02.560
Some can be a little shorter, some are a little&nbsp;
longer, but that's a good average for part one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:10.480
Now, we're going to move on and talk about topics&nbsp;
two and three, which are much harder to predict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:17.600
And you can find sample answers to all four&nbsp;
topics your exam starts with, plus some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:27.040
sample answers from this video in the ultimate&nbsp;
IELTS playbook. This is a free companion PDF&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:34.400
and it will help you prepare not just for IELTS&nbsp;
speaking but for every part of your exam and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:42.720
will link to all the other videos in the ultimate&nbsp;
IELTS guide series. I'll link it below. Now about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:49.760
topics two and three in part one. The examiner&nbsp;
picks them before they even meet you. So it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:58.800
really is the luck. Some are easier to talk about&nbsp;
than others. If you get reading the cinema food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:07.760
you've probably got plenty to say. If you got&nbsp;
perfume or fishing, it's a different story. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:16.640
the strategy is the same either way. Let me show&nbsp;
you two examples. So, let's start with adverbs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:25.120
Imagine the examiner asks, "Are there any ads&nbsp;
you find particularly memorable? Here is a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:32.640
answer." I love adverbs that have a real element&nbsp;
of storytelling. So, it's not just about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:40.240
product being in your face, but maybe there is an&nbsp;
emotion or a feeling or something that resonates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:48.240
with you within the advert. So, that's a good&nbsp;
answer. The vocabulary is natural. The sentences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:58.240
aren't that long, but it sounds fluent because of&nbsp;
one thing in particular. So, in your answer, use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:07.200
phrasal verbs and idiomatic expressions naturally&nbsp;
when you can. Look at these phrases in your face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:16.720
resonate with you. A real element of storytelling.&nbsp;
So these belong to what examiners call idiomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:25.280
language and it's something they specifically&nbsp;
listen for. You don't need big idioms like once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:33.760
in a blue moon. Small everyday phrasal verbs and&nbsp;
expressions count just as much. Now let's take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:41.920
a topic from the harder side of the list. Imagine&nbsp;
you get asked about fishing and you've never been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:51.120
fishing in your life. I asked my friend Scarlet&nbsp;
from England this very question and here is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:59.120
answer she gave. So the question was, "Is fishing&nbsp;
a popular activity in your country?" That's a hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:06.320
question. For me personally, it's not something I&nbsp;
would do, but I'm sure there are a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:14.720
in England who do go fishing. So, notice what she&nbsp;
did here. She bought herself a moment by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:22.560
"That's a hard question." And then she talked&nbsp;
about her own experience briefly and pivoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:30.320
what other people do. So when you don't know much&nbsp;
about a topic, talk about what other people do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:39.280
not yourself. And uh use a few phrases to&nbsp;
give yourself some thinking time. Here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:47.120
a couple. That's an interesting one. I've never&nbsp;
thought about it before. I'm not totally sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:55.600
but I would say to be honest, I haven't paid much&nbsp;
attention to that. But off the top of my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:03.520
I'd say so. Don't abuse these phrases. Don't start&nbsp;
every answer with them. You're not going to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:11.920
high band for using these phrases, but they can&nbsp;
buy you a bit of thinking time to give a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:21.520
answer after. Part one is about yourself and your&nbsp;
experiences. Short questions and short answers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:31.760
The part that really shows your fluency is part&nbsp;
two. So you get a card and the instructions read.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:38.160
You will have to talk about the topic for one&nbsp;
to two minutes. You have one minute to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:46.080
about what you're going to say and you can make&nbsp;
some notes to help you if you wish and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:56.720
read your topic. So, part two is the so-called&nbsp;
long term. The instructions say to talk for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:04.880
to two minutes, but here is one thing to&nbsp;
know. Aim to talk for 2 minutes until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:10.400
examiner stops you. Don't worry about finishing&nbsp;
your answer. I know that sometimes students are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:16.880
worried about whether they'll get a topic&nbsp;
they can actually talk about for 2 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:27.360
So sometimes I ask my students to vote for the&nbsp;
topic they find harder and this is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:37.440
winners, the most dreaded topic I found so far.&nbsp;
Describe a person who likes to grow plants. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:46.000
should say who the person is, how you know the&nbsp;
person, what type of plants he or she grows, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:54.400
explain why growing plants is important for this&nbsp;
person. So maybe you don't know anyone who grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.400 --> 00:14:03.360
plants. Maybe you don't know much about plants&nbsp;
at all, especially in English. So what do you do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:13.680
So, first look at the four bullet points. The&nbsp;
first two are about a person, not about plants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:21.120
So, that's already half of your talk. And even&nbsp;
the third bullet point, it only asks about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:29.040
they grow, not about how they grow them and&nbsp;
like describing the process of growing plants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:38.560
IELTS is not a test of subject knowledge. It's a&nbsp;
test of your English. So if you have some topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:46.640
specific vocabulary, great. If not, work around&nbsp;
it. And here is something you should really know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:56.640
about part two. There are four bullet points,&nbsp;
but they're not equal. The first two are easier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:05.200
who and how you know them. So they need simpler&nbsp;
language. The third and fourth move into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:14.240
explaining what they grow and why it matters to&nbsp;
them. So that's where more complex vocabulary and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:23.120
grammar fit in naturally, especially the last&nbsp;
one. And here is an insider tip from a former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:33.600
IELTS examiner on our team. Make sure you cover&nbsp;
the last point and explain why. Now, let's walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:42.560
through a sample answer step by step. And after&nbsp;
each one, I'll point out what makes it work. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:50.000
I'm going to talk about a neighbor of mine who's a&nbsp;
keen gardener and in fact grows his own vegetables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:58.640
and some beautiful flowers. a simple opening that&nbsp;
goes straight into the story. Notice that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:06.880
already got the phrase a king gardener. That's&nbsp;
someone who's very enthusiastic about gardening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:14.880
He's quite old. He must be in his early 70s, I&nbsp;
imagine, but he's very fit and active for his age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:23.840
He has retired and lives with his wife, who's a&nbsp;
really nice lady. Simple details. age, occupation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:32.800
family. That's all the first bullet needs. And&nbsp;
a natural way to talk about someone's age is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:44.800
his early ' 70s. You can also say mid70s or late&nbsp;
70s or 20s. And uh fit and active for his age is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:54.480
a good everyday collocation. It all sounds very&nbsp;
natural. Now, bullet point two, how you know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.480 --> 00:17:03.280
person. A little tip here. When you need to pick a&nbsp;
person, don't say it's your close relative because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:11.600
otherwise you just say, "Well, it's my mom." Then&nbsp;
what? Pick someone else and then it's much easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:19.920
to describe how you know this person. So, this&nbsp;
bullet point is great for using past tenses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:30.240
and examiners specifically listen for that. So&nbsp;
I first met him when I moved in 6 months ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:37.760
The building has this nice gardens and I saw him&nbsp;
there and we started talking. I told him I knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:45.440
nothing about gardening and that I simply don't&nbsp;
have green fingers. And he laughed and said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:54.400
he would give me a few tips. Now, thanks to him,&nbsp;
I'm the proud owner of two beautiful house plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.400 --> 00:18:05.200
and uh maybe I could even grow my own vegetables.&nbsp;
So, we see a nice mix of past simple verbs, met,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:14.160
moved, saw, started, and so on. And we see some&nbsp;
present comments that connect the past back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:23.040
the present. I'm the proud owner. I could even&nbsp;
grow. And there is a lovely idiomatic expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:31.120
to have green fingers. It means to be naturally&nbsp;
good with plants. Bullet point three requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:39.360
quite specific explanation as to what plants he&nbsp;
or she grows. And there are two ways to go here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:46.000
If you're really advanced and you have a very&nbsp;
wide range of vocabulary, that's your opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:54.080
to show topic specific vocabulary. That would be&nbsp;
a band nine version. If you don't have that kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.080 --> 00:19:00.960
of vocabulary, you can still pass your exam by&nbsp;
giving a much simpler explanation. So, I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:08.320
to show them side by side and present the benign&nbsp;
version. Let's go. My friend has a small patch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:16.400
of his own in the far corner of the gardens. He&nbsp;
grows carrots, potatoes, and onions. He showed me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:24.240
how to sew the seeds for them. There is a lot to&nbsp;
learn about taking care of the soil using natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:31.520
compost as a fertilizer and watering. Of course,&nbsp;
he also tends to the flower beds and uh helps to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:39.200
prune some of the trees we have growing there.&nbsp;
But roses are his specialty. They are in bloom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:46.160
right now and they look absolutely stunning.&nbsp;
Not to mention the fragrance. There is a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:53.440
topic specific vocabulary here. Words like these&nbsp;
really push your score up because the examiner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.440 --> 00:20:01.040
specifically listens for less common vocabulary&nbsp;
connected to the topic. But if you don't have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:07.920
kind of vocabulary, you can still pause and pause&nbsp;
well with a much simpler version on the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:14.160
of the screen. And I'm sure you know almost&nbsp;
all of these words. If you'd like to read it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:20.960
just pause the video now. Now, let's talk about&nbsp;
the most important bullet. Explain why growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:28.480
plants is important for this person. Remember,&nbsp;
always cover the last bullet point in your answer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:37.840
That's where you get to show more complex language&nbsp;
like this. When I asked him whether all this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:44.400
just a hobby or whether he did it to save money&nbsp;
on food, he explained that he just loves doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:52.480
what he does. But he's also a firm believer&nbsp;
in eating natural organically grown food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:58.560
He never buys vegetables from the supermarket&nbsp;
because they're kept in storage for ages and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:05.280
artificially treated to look fresh. Since he told&nbsp;
me that, I've started thinking a lot more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:16.560
what I eat. Notice the linkers when, but because,&nbsp;
since they're connecting the ideas together. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:24.320
we also have some collocations. A firm believer,&nbsp;
organically grown, kept in storage, artificially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:31.600
treated. And this is a very strong answer. When&nbsp;
you're explaining why someone does something or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:39.760
why they feel a certain way about something, you&nbsp;
naturally reach out for more complex vocabulary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:47.280
and grammar. Now, let's talk about the part&nbsp;
where the examiner really pushes you to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:55.200
out how good your English is. I am speaking part&nbsp;
three. Part three lasts between four and five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.200 --> 00:22:02.480
minutes just like part one and the examiner will&nbsp;
ask you between five and six main questions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:09.840
some smaller questions to help you develop your&nbsp;
answers. There are some important differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:16.720
between part one and part three that you should&nbsp;
really know about to give the examiner what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:24.480
expect from you for a high score. So, in part&nbsp;
three, the questions are longer, more general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:30.640
and more abstract. And the examiner has more&nbsp;
freedom, too. They're not just reading from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:37.120
list like in part one. And there is one thing&nbsp;
in particular that catches students out. Part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:46.400
one is about you, what you do, what you think,&nbsp;
your experiences. Part three is about people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:55.040
general society ideas. If you start talking about&nbsp;
your own life, the examiner will likely interrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.040 --> 00:23:02.880
you and redirect you. In part three, there are&nbsp;
three sets of questions for the examiner to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:12.800
from. Set one is easier. Set three is the hardest.&nbsp;
And here is the part that surprises many students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:20.720
The harder your questions in part three, the&nbsp;
better. That's because examiners choose set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:28.720
one and two for candidates around a band five to&nbsp;
six. And for candidates likely to score a seven or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:37.840
higher, they choose sets two and three. So if your&nbsp;
examiner is asking you tough questions, that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:45.040
good sign. They think you can handle them. Let's&nbsp;
look at one question and a sample answer from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:54.560
each set. The examiner will start this part by&nbsp;
reminding you about your part two topic like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.560 --> 00:24:01.760
You've been talking about a famous person who is a&nbsp;
good role model for younger people and I'd like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:09.440
ask you some more general questions about this.&nbsp;
And then they ask you the first question. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:16.560
question is from set one. Let's first consider&nbsp;
becoming famous. Can you tell me what types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:26.400
of people tend to become famous in your country?&nbsp;
This is one of the easier types of questions. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:35.440
asking you to describe and classify. So listen to&nbsp;
this answer and see how I expand it to be longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:41.200
When I think about famous people, what comes to&nbsp;
my mind are celebrities from the entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:50.320
industry like actors and musicians who are always&nbsp;
in the spotlight. Athletes are also a big deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:58.800
especially those who excel in popular sports like&nbsp;
football and tennis. Uh recently, social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:05.760
influencers and content creators have become&nbsp;
really popular, too. So it seems people from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:12.960
different walks of life can become famous here.&nbsp;
If in part one our answers were 20 or 25 seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:22.240
long, in part three you can talk for 45 seconds&nbsp;
at a time and giving examples is one of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:29.840
ways to extend your answers in part three. So&nbsp;
we talk a little about actors and musicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:38.560
then we add athletes, then we add influences,&nbsp;
and each example adds another sentence. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:46.000
is also some lovely idiomatic language. Come to&nbsp;
my mind, in the spotlight, a big deal, different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:54.400
walks of life. Now, you might be thinking, "My&nbsp;
answers aren't usually this long." And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.400 --> 00:26:02.400
fine. If your answer is shorter, the examiner&nbsp;
will ask follow-up questions. That's normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:10.640
not a bad sign. They're trained to do that. But&nbsp;
still try to give an extended answer every time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:17.520
Now, let's move to the second set of questions and&nbsp;
the examiner will tell you their switching focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:25.600
Maybe like this. Let's go on to talk about the&nbsp;
importance of role models. How important is it for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:35.440
young people to have good role models? So, set two&nbsp;
questions ask you to analyze, agree or disagree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:43.200
or weigh things up. And uh here is an answer&nbsp;
that does exactly that. I think it's important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:51.360
but maybe not as critical as people sometimes&nbsp;
say. On the one hand, having someone to look up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:58.160
to can really shape the choices young people make,&nbsp;
especially when it comes to things like career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:05.280
lifestyle, or how they treat other people. On&nbsp;
the other hand, young people are also influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:12.880
by their families, their friends, and their own&nbsp;
experiences. So, role models are only one part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:20.160
the picture. A couple of things to notice. First,&nbsp;
this answer doesn't fully agree with the question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:27.680
Don't be afraid to disagree with the examiner's&nbsp;
question, but do it politely. Here, the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:36.480
starts with, "I think it's important, but maybe&nbsp;
not as critical as people sometimes say." So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:44.480
that's a soft disagreement. The examiner doesn't&nbsp;
mind at all. There are no right or wrong opinions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:53.520
Second, look at the structure on the one hand and&nbsp;
on the other hand. That's a good way to organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.520 --> 00:28:02.400
opposing ideas. And it's good in part three.&nbsp;
It's really a simple way to extend your answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:10.400
and show that you can weigh up different sides.&nbsp;
And now set three, the most challenging one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:18.000
These questions are designed to push you to&nbsp;
your limits. For example, could it be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:27.120
that celebrity culture will one day be seen as&nbsp;
the defining characteristic of the 21st century?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:35.760
That's a tough one. It's long abstract as the&nbsp;
word the phrase defining characteristic. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:44.080
does it mean? So, here is an important tip. If you&nbsp;
don't understand a word in part three, you can ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:52.880
the examiner. Just say something like, "I'm sorry,&nbsp;
what do you mean by defining characteristic?" Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.880 --> 00:29:01.120
"Do you mean the main feature of this century?"&nbsp;
In part three, you can ask the examiner to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:09.120
what a certain word means, or you can ask to&nbsp;
repeat the question. In part one, you can only ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:16.720
to repeat the question. Okay? Once you understand&nbsp;
what they're asking, give the answer. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:23.200
to tell you the truth, this is something I've&nbsp;
never given very much thought to, but I guess it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:30.720
possible. Maybe not so much where I come from, but&nbsp;
certainly in some cultures like the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:38.000
or in parts of Western Europe, even political&nbsp;
leaders are often treated like celebrities. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:47.040
sport, it's the same. So yes, it might well be one&nbsp;
of the things future generations look back on. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:53.920
answer starts with a filler to tell you the truth&nbsp;
and admits the candidate hasn't thought about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.560 --> 00:30:02.800
That's completely fine. Again, try not to use&nbsp;
it every time, but if you really have to think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:09.600
keep on talking. After that, still try to&nbsp;
answer the question directly. And if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:15.840
examiner feels you're going off topic, they&nbsp;
will interrupt you and probably ask the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:21.840
question again on just move on to the&nbsp;
next question. And that's part three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:28.000
So when you hear the examiner say, "Thank you.&nbsp;
That's the end of this speaking test," I promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:35.040
you, you'll be relieved. So that's the full&nbsp;
speaking test from the first ID questions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:41.360
the final thank you. And the more familiar&nbsp;
this feels before you walk into the exam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:47.280
the more confident you'll be when it actually&nbsp;
happens. Don't forget to download the Ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:54.800
Ayats playbook using the link below. It's got&nbsp;
the sample answers for the topics like hometown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.800 --> 00:31:02.400
work, studies, and our part two topic from&nbsp;
this video too and some extra materials for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:10.400
the rest of the test as we release them. And now&nbsp;
